Now we can see the issue clearly. You have multiple Auth CLI packages installed globally, and one of them is likely creating the conflicting auth command.

You currently have:

@7edge/auth-cli -> local Frontend/AuthPlatform/auth-cli
@akhileshb/auth-cli@
auth-cli@1.0.0
authplatform@1.0.0

Also, which auth returns nothing, so the auth executable isn't currently being resolved through your PATH.

Do this cleanly

First uninstall all the Auth-related global packages:

npm uninstall -g @7edge/auth-cli @akhileshb/auth-cli auth-cli authplatform

Then check:

npm list -g --depth=0

Now check whether the old executable still exists:

ls -l ~/.nvm/versions/node/v20.19.6/bin/auth

If it says:

No such file or directory

that's good.

If it still exists, remove it:

rm ~/.nvm/versions/node/v20.19.6/bin/auth
Then install only your new package
npm install -g @akhileshb/auth-cli@latest

Check:

npm list -g --depth=0

You should see:

@akhileshb/auth-cli@1.0.1

Then:

which auth
auth --help
One important thing

This entry:

@7edge/auth-cli@npm:@akhileshb/auth-cli@1.0.1 -> ./../../../../../Frontend/AuthPlatform/auth-cli

means you previously installed/linked the package from your local Frontend/AuthPlatform/auth-cli directory. That's probably why you have multiple versions/links hanging around.

If your goal now is to test the published npm package, removing those local/global versions and installing only:

npm install -g @akhileshb/auth-cli@latest

is the cleanest approach.